; =====================================================================
;  SNAKE in hand-written ARM64 machine code for macOS on Apple Silicon
; =====================================================================
;  Every byte of the program was written by hand as hex, in file order,
;  then a script rewrote each hex byte as the 8 binary digits below.
;  No compiler, assembler or linker was used. The assembly text in the
;  comments is for the reader only; the build ignores it.
;
;  Format:  each byte is 8 binary digits, ';' starts a comment,
;           '@XXXX' pads with zero bytes up to file offset 0xXXXX (hex).
;  Build:   python3 build.py      (bits -> bytes, then ad-hoc codesign)
;  Play:    ./snake               w a s d or arrow keys, q to quit
;
;  ARM64 is little-endian, so each 32-bit instruction word appears
;  with its bytes reversed. sub sp, sp, #0x800 is the word
;      11010001 00100000 00000011 11111111
;  and is written below as
;      11111111 00000011 00100000 11010001
; =====================================================================

; ---------------- Mach-O 64 header (32 bytes) ------------------------
11001111 11111010 11101101 11111110  ; magic 0xfeedfacf (64-bit Mach-O)
00001100 00000000 00000000 00000001  ; cputype  CPU_TYPE_ARM64
00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000  ; cpusubtype ALL
00000010 00000000 00000000 00000000  ; filetype MH_EXECUTE
00001001 00000000 00000000 00000000  ; ncmds = 9
00011000 00000010 00000000 00000000  ; sizeofcmds = 0x218
10000101 00000000 00100000 00000000  ; flags NOUNDEFS|DYLDLINK|TWOLEVEL|PIE
00000000 00000000 00000000 00000000  ; reserved

